---
import Entry from '../../layouts/Entry.astro';
import Note from '../../components/Note.astro';
import Plate from '../../components/Plate.astro';
import Specimen from '../../components/Specimen.astro';
---
<Entry
  slug="qb-clutch-optimality"
  links={[{ href: 'https://github.com/EshaanDhavala/qb-optimality', label: 'Code on GitHub' }]}
>
  <h2><span class="label">The question</span>Who stays composed when the pocket collapses and the game is close?</h2>
  <p>
    “Clutch” arguments usually rest on highlight throws. We wanted something measurable. Given what the
    pocket actually looked like on a play (where the rushers were, how fast the space was shrinking, how long
    the QB held the ball), how much better or worse did the play turn out than a typical QB would manage?
  </p>
  <p>
    The idea extends Brian Burke’s DeepQB (MIT Sloan, 2019) from throw choice to pocket behavior.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="label">My part</span>Turning tracking data into pocket geometry, then a model</h2>
  <Note>The pocket is literally a convex hull: the shape around every pass blocker, measured at the snap and again at the throw.</Note>
  <p>
    Using 10 Hz player tracking from the NFL Big Data Bowl (weeks 1–8 of 2021), I engineered the
    pocket-geometry features and trained the model:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pocket area at release</strong> and <strong>collapse rate</strong>, from the convex hull of all pass blockers at the snap vs. at the throw.</li>
    <li><strong>Pressure:</strong> distance to the nearest rusher, the approach speed of the two closest rushers, and rushers within 3 yards.</li>
    <li><strong>The QB:</strong> drop depth, speed and body orientation at release, time to throw.</li>
    <li><strong>Game context:</strong> down, distance, field position, clock, score, win probability.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    An XGBoost regressor predicts expected points added (EPA) for each dropback. A QB’s <em>optimality</em> on a play is actual EPA minus predicted. The <em>clutch rating</em> compares optimality in high-leverage
    moments (win probability 40–60%) against everything else. Validation is leave-one-week-out, so the model
    never sees the week it’s scoring.
  </p>

  <Plate fig="1" wide>
    <div slot="media" id="qb-chart" class="chart" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of clutch rating for 15 quarterbacks, from Justin Herbert at +0.53 to Carson Wentz at −0.50"></div>
    Clutch rating = mean optimality in close games − mean optimality otherwise (EPA per play). Bar labels show
    the number of clutch dropbacks: these are small samples from half a season, so treat it as a first look,
    not a verdict.
  </Plate>

  <Specimen rows={[
    ['Dropbacks', '7,088'],
    ['Features', '19'],
    ['RMSE (leave-one-week-out)', '1.57 EPA'],
    ['Model', 'XGBoost, 200 trees, depth 3'],
  ]} />

  <h2><span class="label">Honest caveats</span>What this can’t say yet</h2>
  <p>
    EPA is noisy, and the tracking data covers only eight weeks, so several QBs have under 20 clutch
    dropbacks. The next steps are bootstrapped intervals on each rating and more seasons of tracking data.
  </p>
</Entry>

<script>
  import * as Plot from '@observablehq/plot';

  type Row = { passer_player_name: string; clutch_rating: number; n_clutch_plays: number };

  const el = document.getElementById('qb-chart')!;
  const css = (v: string) => getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(v).trim();

  const rows: Row[] = await fetch('/data/qb_clutch_ratings.csv')
    .then((r) => r.text())
    .then((t) => {
      const [head, ...lines] = t.trim().split('\n');
      const cols = head.split(',');
      return lines.map((l) => {
        const v = l.split(',');
        const o: any = Object.fromEntries(cols.map((c, i) => [c, v[i]]));
        return { passer_player_name: o.passer_player_name, clutch_rating: +o.clutch_rating, n_clutch_plays: +o.n_clutch_plays };
      });
    });

  function draw() {
    const ink = css('--ink'), pen = css('--pen'), rule = css('--rule'), pencil = css('--pencil');
    const chart = Plot.plot({
      width: Math.min(el.clientWidth || 640, 820),
      height: 440,
      marginLeft: 100,
      marginRight: 40,
      style: { background: 'transparent', color: ink, fontFamily: 'IBM Plex Mono, monospace', fontSize: '11px' },
      x: { label: null, grid: true, domain: [-0.6, 0.6] },
      y: { label: null, domain: [...rows].sort((a, b) => b.clutch_rating - a.clutch_rating).map((d) => d.passer_player_name) },
      marks: [
        Plot.barX(rows, { x: 'clutch_rating', y: 'passer_player_name', fill: (d: Row) => (d.clutch_rating >= 0 ? pen : rule) }),
        Plot.ruleX([0], { stroke: ink }),
        Plot.text(rows, {
          x: () => 0.6,
          y: 'passer_player_name',
          text: (d: Row) => `n=${d.n_clutch_plays}`,
          textAnchor: 'end',
          fill: pencil,
        }),
        Plot.tip(rows, Plot.pointerY({ x: 'clutch_rating', y: 'passer_player_name', title: (d: Row) => `${d.passer_player_name}\nclutch rating ${d.clutch_rating.toFixed(2)}\n${d.n_clutch_plays} clutch dropbacks` })),
      ],
    });
    el.replaceChildren(chart);
  }
  draw();
  window.addEventListener('themechange', draw);
  matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').addEventListener('change', draw);
  let t: number;
  window.addEventListener('resize', () => { clearTimeout(t); t = window.setTimeout(draw, 150); });
</script>

<style>
  .chart { min-height: 440px; overflow-x: auto; }
</style>
